package agentsdoc

import "strings"

// writeV04cSection documents what 0.4c adds to composition for an authoring
// agent: the static props of a template (`props` on `<template>`, the
// attributes of a `<use>`, `{$name}`) and its one default slot (`<slot/>` and
// the content of a `<use>`), with the conventions that choose between
// `with`, a prop, and the slot. It follows the v0.4b composition section it
// extends and names no path of the repository, since the project briefing
// (ProjectMarkdown) is this same text. The tool output for placed content
// (`inspect`, `ir`) is in the v0.4a tools section.
func writeV04cSection(b *strings.Builder) {
	b.WriteString("### v0.4c: props and the slot\n\n")
	b.WriteString("- A template's **props** are named pieces of text that each `<use>` of it passes and its body reads as `{$name}`. `<template id=\"T\" props=\"title max\">` declares them: the names are separated by exactly one space (none before the first or after the last), each follows `[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*`, none is written twice, and none is `id`, `template`, `with`, `if`, `hidden`, or `class`, which a `<use>` keeps for itself (each mistake is `V003` at the `props`, and `props=\"\"` or no `props` declares none). A `<use>` passes a prop as an attribute with its name, `<use template=\"T\" title=\"CPU\" max=\"100\"/>`, and the value is literal text, taken as written; `title=\"\"` passes the empty string, a value. Any other attribute on a `<use>` is `V002`, and for a name that could be a prop its message says to declare it in `props`. Props are replaced when the document is read, before any data, so they cost nothing at run time and never change while the app runs; the dump shows the copy as substituted.\n")
	b.WriteString("- `{$name}` may stand only in these places of a template's body: the text of a `<text>`, `<button>`, or `<column>`; the attributes that take a `{path}` template (`title`, `placeholder`, the `label` of a `button`, `checkbox`, `select`, or `tab`, a tab's `short`); the attributes with a literal value (`width`, `height`, `gap`, `pad`, `border`, `min`, `max`, `mark`, `value`, `wrap`, `axis`, `priority`, `scale`, `class`, `style`, and the action name of an `on:*`); and the value of a prop that a `<use>` in the body passes on. Several may share a value with literal text or `{path}`: `class=\"k-stat {$tone}\"`, `style=\"width: {$w}\"`, `title=\"{$label}: {f.value}\"`. Anywhere else a `{$name}` is `V003`, and inside a template its attribute is ignored in the template and in every copy: in an attribute that takes a path (`bind`, `each`, `key`, `checked`, `if`, `hidden`, `disabled`, `open`, `class:NAME`, a use's `with`), in an `id` or a `#id` reference (`focus`), in a use's `template` or an include's `src`, in `focusable`, `secret`, `single`, and the like, and inside braces (`{f.{$k}}`); so is a `{$name}` that names no declared prop, or a malformed one (`{$Title}`, `{$}`). Outside a template's body every `{$name}` is `V003` too (`props exist only in a template's body`), but the value is then read as written, so the dump keeps the literal `{$title}`. An attribute name never holds one (`class:{$p}` is `V005`).\n")
	b.WriteString("- A prop the use does not pass: an attribute whose whole value is exactly `{$name}` is not in the copy, as if the template had not written it (`<sparkline min=\"0\" max=\"{$max}\"/>` without `max` takes its range from its values, and `class=\"{$tone}\"` writes no class); anywhere else `{$name}` is the empty string (`title=\"{$title}: total\"` becomes `: total`). \"Whole value\" is exact: `max=\" {$max}\"` becomes `max=\" \"`, which is then checked as written. A prop is optional, then, unless the template needs it.\n")
	b.WriteString("- Props are text, never data. A pair of braces that a prop's value makes is `V003` (`prop \"title\" makes the pair \"{user.name}\": a prop is text; pass a path with with=\"…\"`) and that prop is not passed in that copy; data goes through `with`. A lone `{` or `}` stays literal, and substitution is one pass: the text a value brings is never read as another `{$name}`. A substituted value is checked as if written by hand (`max=\"lots\"` is the bad-`max` `V003`, `border=\"wavy\"` the bad-`border` one), at the template's attribute, and its message ends with `, placed by <use …> at FILE:LINE:COL` naming the use that wrote the text. The action name of an `on:*` that holds a prop fires the substituted name.\n")
	b.WriteString("- A `<use>` in a template's body may pass that template's props on: `<use template=\"field\" label=\"{$label}\"/>`. When the outer use does not pass `label`, the attribute is not in the inner use, which then does not pass it either. A use's `class` may hold a prop (`class=\"{$tone}\"`); its `id`, `template`, `with`, `if`, and `hidden` cannot, and a use that holds one inserts nothing.\n")
	b.WriteString("- A template's **slot** is where each use puts the content written between its tags. `<slot/>` takes no attribute (`V002`) and no content (`V013`), there is no fallback (a use with no content leaves it empty, and a parent whose only element is the slot is `:empty`), and a template has one: each further `<slot/>` is `V019` and receives nothing. A slot may stand directly in the template, in a container, a `modal`, a `tab`, a `tabs`, a static `list` (one without `each`), or in the content of a `<use>` in the template (forwarding, below). It is `V001` outside a template's body, inside a list `<item>`, in the template of an `each` container, directly in a `<table>` or a `<list each=…>` (their content repeats per row and would need the row's alias), and in the content of a `<use>` when an `<include>` brings it there (write it in the content itself). A slot that is reported receives nothing.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The content of a `<use>`, its **fill**, is the elements between `<use …>` and `</use>`, in order (comments and whitespace are dropped); an empty `<use/>` has an empty fill. Text directly in a use whose template has a slot is `V013` (`wrap it in <text>`), and any content for a template with no `<slot/>` is one `V013` (`<template id=\"T\"> has no <slot/>`), checked as written whether or not the use expands. A `tui`, `screen`, `style`, `keymap`, `bind`, or `template` in it is `V001`. The fill is part of the copy: it stands where the slot stands, in the focus cycle, the cascade, and the dump, and its parent there checks it (`tab` elements fill a slot that stands in a `tabs`; a `tab` elsewhere is `V016`). The use's `if` or `hidden` removes the fill with the copy.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The fill belongs to the place where it is written, not to the use: its paths resolve there, without the aliases of the use's `with` and without the `each` aliases of the copy (an alias of the place where it is written, such as a list row's, still is in scope); its ids are not scoped by the use (`<input id=\"note\">` in the content of `<use id=\"side\">` is `note`, `#note` in a keymap, while the template's own ids are `side__x`); its `#id` references and its `<include src>` mean what they would if the elements stood in place of the use (an include looks for its `src` next to the file that writes the content, and a fragment that includes itself that way is `V006`); a `{$name}` in it reads the props of the template whose body holds it, and is `V003` outside a template; the use's static `class` does not reach it. Its ids are ids of the document (a repeat is `V004`), and they do not count toward the use's `V012`, which only the template's own ids set off. Its elements count toward the expansion limits once per place a slot puts them, and a diagnostic about them carries the suffix of the place where they are written.\n")
	b.WriteString("- A template passes its content to a smaller one by putting its own `<slot/>` in the content of the inner use: `<use template=\"panel\" title=\"{$title}\"><text>Notes</text><slot/></use>`. The content keeps the meaning of the place where the outer use is written, through any number of templates, and the elements around the forwarded slot belong to the template that wrote them.\n")
	b.WriteString("- Conventions for props and the slot:\n")
	b.WriteString("  - Pass literal text as a prop and data through `with`: `<use template=\"stat\" with=\"load.cpu as s\" title=\"CPU\" max=\"100\"/>` reads its numbers live and spells its title and limit once. A prop is never a path (`title=\"{user.name}\"` is `V003`): give the copy `with=\"user as u\"` and write `{u.name}`, or put a `<text>` in the slot.\n")
	b.WriteString("  - Put a body in the slot, not in a prop or a `with`: markup that the use writes keeps the paths, the unscoped ids (`#note`), and the actions of the place where it is written, so a panel, dialog, or card template wraps what its users put inside it. Content for a table's columns or a list's rows cannot come from a slot.\n")
	b.WriteString("  - Size or vary a copy with a `style` prop: declare `style` in `props`, write `style=\"{$style}\"` on the template's root, and each use passes `style=\"width: 18\"` or none (an unpassed whole-value prop writes no `style`, so the root's other rules apply). A reusable template takes a `style` prop for its root this way. For a look the stylesheet owns, use a static `class` on the use instead.\n")
	b.WriteString("  - A prop is optional when it is the whole value of an attribute: if a use does not pass it, the attribute is not written (a whole-value `{$tone}` writes no class, so that copy is the plain one); in running text it becomes empty.\n")
	b.WriteString("  - Read a diagnostic about a substituted value by its end: `, placed by <use …> at FILE:LINE:COL` names the use that wrote the text, and the location before it is the template's own attribute.\n\n")
}
